==German==

===Etymology===
Probably related to {{term|Zinke||point, prong|lang=de}}, from {{etyl|goh|de}} {{term|zint||a jag, point|lang=goh}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|de}} {{m|gem-pro|*tinduz}}, {{m|gem-pro|*tindaz||prong, pinnacle}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|de}} {{m|ine-pro|*(e)dont-||tooth, projection}}, see also Dutch {{term|tinne||battlement|lang=nl}}, {{etyl|de|-}} {{term|Zinne||pinnacle, battlement|lang=de}}, {{etyl|da|-}} {{term|tinde||pinnacle, battlement|lang=da}}, {{etyl|sv|-}} {{term|tinne||tooth of a rake|lang=sv}}, {{etyl|is|-}} {{term|tindur||spike, tooth of a rake or harrow, pinnacle, peak, battlement|lang=is}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/tsɪŋk/|lang=de}}
